That's a dual edged sword. Answers to the following questions are spoilers.

I'll give you some spoiler space before answering:


































>1) When you are bitten by werejackal for example, are you screwed for 
>life?

No.

> Are there things you can do to cure yourself or prevent being 
>bitten short of running away or teleporting?

Yes. Holy water or praying to your god will purify you. Also a sprig of
wolfsbane will cure lycanthopy.

>	2) Use of sinks and fountains: Should I drink? Not drink?

No. They are bad news. The bad effects far outweigh the good ones.

> What are 
>benefits and disadvantages? Does it help at all with hunger? What should 
>I dip?

Same for dipping. There is a better way that's much less dangerous. I'll
spoil it to you if you ask again here.

>3) Praying: I know it helps with hunger. Though when I prayed a second 
>time the god was displeased. Is praying a last resort option?

No. It's extremely helpful during the game. However gods don't like
whiney petitioners. So you need to wait awhile between prayers. In the
early game usually prayers every 500-600 turns will do you fine.

> How does it work?

If you really want to know read this spoiler:

http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/pray.html

> What does it help with?

Everything dude. Nethack gods are not gods for nothing. They can cure you,
add hitpoints, feed you, give you stuff, make holy water (which is turbo
juice in the game), and much much more.

>4) Altars: I know it lets you identify cursed/blessed items. Any other 
>uses?

Many. First is sacrificing. Second is conversion to holy water. Altars 
are where most of the really good effects that gods can bestow upon you
occur. And you can find out your status with your god in respect to
praying at an altar.

> Obviously type of altar (lawful, etc.) matters. How so?

An Altar to your god is a good thing. Altars for other gods can be a rocky
road. You don't want to screw it up and piss off your god. That's a really
bad place to be.

If you want to know more lookup 'altar conversion' on Google.

>5) Being threatened: What does it mean? This happens sometimes when I 
>read spellbooks.

Just a feeling. But you don't get the spell. That's the bad effect.

> Also, is it bad to try to read books you cannot easily 
>comprehend (game warning)?

It depends. First off if you're having problems reading the spell, you're
probably going to have problems casting the spell too.

There's a solution to the reading problem. However you need to ask for
that spoiler.

>	6) Scrolls, spellbooks, potions: The names of these are randomized 
>every game. Correct? So I've been just using/reading these to figure out 
>what they do every game. Obviously, I end up not using most of them 
>because I'm afraid of negative effects. Are there ways of identifying 
>these short of scroll of identifications (which needs to be identified 
>itself first :) )?

Yes. Here are some tips:

1) Shops. Everything has a specific price. You can identify lots of stuff
just knowing how much it costs.

2) Wands. Engraving with them can help ID them without all the nasty 
effects.

3) Potions. You and throw them at monsters and see the effects. However
I'm really stingy with my potions because holy water is more valuable than
half of the potions in the game. I usually use ID and shops to determine
what potions are useful, and which are useless.

>7) You have some attributes of your character that are displayed when 
>you die: You were stealthy, you were a werejackal, you were fast, etc. 
>Is there a way to see these during the game?

There's a potion, a wand, and a tool that will give you that information.
The potion is enlightenment. The wand is probing, and the tool is a
stethoscope.

> I noticed my monk became 
>stealthy (not sure what it does) when he reached level 5 for example. 

Monsters that are sleeping ignore you if you are stealthy.

>So, I guess you gain these attributes though I don't know according to what.

Most are given according to level of the character. Monks get instrinsic,
which are what they are called, every other level.

>8) I don't know some of the miscellaneous commands in the game do or 
>how/why I should use them. Among these are: #invoke, #offer, #rub, #sit, 
>#wipe.

#invoke: invokes a special power of an item. You need a powerful item to 
         do this.

#offer : sacrifice a fresh corpse to you god.

#rub   : used for some stones and some lamps.

#sit   : sit on something. Usually a throne.

#wipe  : wipe your face.

>9) I don't know some of the miscellaneous items in the game do or 
>how/why I should use them. Among these are: magic marker, towel (wipe 
>but what), stethescope, looking glass. There are some others but that's 
>all I can think of now.

magic marker: Makes spells and spellbooks from blanks. Really useful.

towel       : dual function. Can blind you like a blindfold (put it on)
              or wipe your face (apply it)

stethoscope : discussed earlier.

looking glass: It's a mirror.


